                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the commercial operating model`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Thornbury's three largest accounts (£19M, 13% of revenue) have mandated punch-out catalogues, real-time stock/ETA, self-service reordering and API integration from <strong>1 October 2026</strong> — and you can't meet a single requirement today. With ~£22M of mid-market accounts watching, <strong>~28% of revenue (~£41M)</strong> is exposed within 12 months. Only 18 of 95 reps are digitally confident; 41 actively resist. This is a <strong>leadership and sequencing problem, not a software purchase</strong>: technology, capability and operating-model must move together. Diagnose where value is created vs where cost-to-serve is highest, name the most exposed segments — and decide how hard the deadline really is. <em>Treat it as a target and the £9M account triggers its switching clause.</em>`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Sequence the £4.5M roadmap`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Allocate the <strong>£4.5M</strong> across four levers — commerce platform & e-procurement, CRM & analytics, rep reskilling & change, and inside-sales/digital channel. Full scope costs <strong>~£7.8M (1.7× budget)</strong>, so you must choose what to defer. The mandate forces <strong>platform-first</strong> to hit 1 October — but a platform with starved reskilling is a £2.4M tool nobody uses: three months in, adoption is 9% and your reps key orders by phone to protect their commission. <em>Never let the technology run ahead of the people who sell with it.</em> Publish a sequence and a logic for what comes first.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Reskill the team & confront channel conflict`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The hard human round. Design the <strong>coverage model</strong> — transactional reorders belong in low-cost digital, but migrate your complex top-3 strategic accounts to self-service and you break the ones that most need a human. Redesign the <strong>commission plan</strong>: 55% of rep variable pay is reorder commission, so an unchanged plan pays reps to steer customers off the portal — your own incentives fighting your transformation. And set a <strong>channel-governance rule</strong>: open a marketplace at list minus 8% with no pricing floor and your £9M account demands the same discount — <em>a price war with yourself</em>. Defend it to the field-rep council.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Recover & prove ROI`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Six months in: adoption is uneven, the <strong>£9M account is wavering</strong> — one missed integration from triggering its switching clause — and the CFO wants the value case. Reallocate the remaining budget where adoption actually stalled, decide whether to send a field-led task-force to finish the £9M integration, and build the board-ready case. The CFO approved £4.5M and will not accept “platform delivered, 60 reps trained” — <em>tell them revenue retained, cost-to-serve change and payback on the £4.5M</em>, with the path to scale.`,

