                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_coordinated:   `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> Matcham's point exactly: transformation is realised only when technology, capability and operating-model move together. Platform-first to hit the mandate, but the people right behind it — that's the frame that turns spend into adoption.`,
                    coach_r1_techbuy:       `<strong>“Buy the best platform and the rest follows” is Common Error number one.</strong> The technology is the easy part. Without reskilling and an operating-model change, you'll own a punch-out catalogue your reps route customers away from. What changes the day after the platform goes live?`,
                    coach_r1_defend:        `<strong>Protecting the reps by resisting self-service protects nothing.</strong> The mandate is the customer's, not yours — the £9M account will simply leave. Defend the relationships by re-skilling the reps for the accounts that need them, not by refusing the channel the customer demands.`,
                    coach_r1_cost:          `<strong>Pushing all 6,400 accounts to digital is the cost trap.</strong> You'll digitise the complex, high-value relationships that most need a human and break them. Cost-to-serve matters, but coverage is decided by value first — not cost alone.`,
                    coach_r1_smallest:      `<strong>The smallest accounts aren't the exposure — the mandated ones are.</strong> The £19M top-3 plus the ~£22M watchers are ~28% of revenue and they have a deadline. Name them, or you'll optimise cost-to-serve on accounts nobody threatened to take.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You framed it as a coordinated change AND named the real exposure — the mandated £19M plus the £22M watchers, ~28% of revenue on a hard deadline. That's the lens that makes the whole sequence add up.`,
                    coach_r1_softdeadline:  `<strong>Treat 1 October as a soft target and it will cost you the anchor.</strong> It's a hard customer deadline: miss the punch-out integration and the £9M account triggers its switching clause and a second mandate wave follows. What does a hard deadline cost when you treat it as a wish?`,
                    coach_r2_platformfirst: `<strong>Platform-first with reskilling right behind is the sequence.</strong> You hit the mandate AND you don't strand the technology. Each wave funds and de-risks the next — that's transformation sequencing under a binding budget done properly.`,
                    coach_r2_techthenlater: `<strong>Deferring reskilling to “later, once the tools are in” is the classic strand.</strong> The tools go in, nobody is trained or incentivised to use them, and adoption sits at 9%. Capability has to land with the platform, not a wave behind it.`,
                    coach_r2_peoplefirst:   `<strong>Reskilling first sounds principled, but it misses the deadline.</strong> The platform isn't live by 1 October, the £9M account walks, and a beautifully-trained team has nothing to sell with. The mandate forces platform-first — sequence the people right behind it.`,
                    coach_r2_stranded:      `<strong>You spent big on the platform and starved the reskilling.</strong> Three months in, the punch-out catalogue is live — and digital adoption is 9%, your reps still key orders by phone to protect commission, and the £9M account says it's “no different.” You bought a £2.4M platform nobody uses. Which should have come first — the technology or the people who sell with it?`,
                    coach_r3_valuebased:    `<strong>Value-based coverage is the right model.</strong> Transactional reorders go to low-cost digital; the complex, high-value accounts that most need a human stay field-led. You cut cost-to-serve where it's safe and protect the relationships where it isn't.`,
                    coach_r3_alldigital:    `<strong>You migrated the top-3 strategic accounts to self-service to save cost.</strong> Two just escalated — their complex, configured orders now generate errors and no one is managing the relationship. You've digitised the accounts that most needed a human. Segment coverage by value, not just by cost.`,
                    coach_r3_nochange:      `<strong>Keeping every account field-led to keep the reps happy means nothing changes.</strong> Cost-to-serve stays high, the mandated accounts still can't self-serve, and you've spent the budget for a model you didn't deploy. Coverage has to actually move.`,
                    coach_r3_neutral:       `<strong>A channel-neutral plan is what unblocks adoption.</strong> Reps now earn on the account whichever way the order comes — so they steer customers to the right route instead of quietly defending phone reorders. Your incentives finally pull the same way as your transformation.`,
                    coach_r3_commission:    `<strong>You launched self-service and left the commission plan alone.</strong> Your reps just realised 55% of their variable pay was reorder commission — and they're quietly steering customers off the portal to protect it. Your own incentive plan is fighting your transformation. What do you change?`,
                    coach_r3_cut:           `<strong>Stripping reorder commission to fund the platform punishes the people you need.</strong> The reps absorb a pay cut for a change they didn't ask for — the resistant 41 become 60, and adoption stalls on the very people meant to drive it. Re-align the incentive, don't just cut it.`,
                    coach_r3_pricewar:      `<strong>Your marketplace listing went live at list minus 8% to win volume.</strong> Your largest field-managed account just found it and is demanding the same discount on £9M of spend. You've started a price war with yourself. The governance rule should have existed before you opened the channel.`,
                    coach_r4_enablement:    `<strong>Reallocating into enablement is exactly where uneven adoption gets fixed.</strong> Coaching, in-portal nudges and account-team support land the behaviour change the platform alone never could. This is enablement, not just training — and it's what moves the adoption line.`,
                    coach_r4_features:      `<strong>More features won't fix adoption.</strong> The problem isn't functionality, it's behaviour — reps and customers aren't using what you already built. Pouring the remaining budget into the platform repeats the strand instead of curing it.`,
                    coach_r4_discount:      `<strong>Discounting on the marketplace to buy back the £9M account reopens the price war.</strong> You'd retain the volume at a structurally lower margin and teach every other account to demand the same. Win the account back with service and integration, not a discount you can't unwind.`,
                    coach_r4_lostaccount:   `<strong>It's 2 October and the £9M account triggered its switching clause yesterday.</strong> The punch-out integration slipped, you didn't send the task-force, and the first orders have moved to a rival. Two mid-market accounts are now asking the same questions. A hard customer deadline treated as soft just cost you the anchor.`,
                    coach_r4_roi:           `<strong>That's the case the CFO approved £4.5M for.</strong> Revenue retained, cost-to-serve down, and a defensible payback with the path to scale — plus you sent the task-force and held the £9M account. You've argued the transformation in the language of the board, not a list of activities.`,
                    coach_r4_activity:      `<strong>“Platform delivered, CRM deployed, 60 reps trained” — I'm the CFO, and that's not what I asked.</strong> I approved £4.5M. Tell me how much revenue we retained, what happened to cost-to-serve, and when I get my money back. Transformation is argued in ROI, not activity.`,
                    coach_r4_vision:        `<strong>A bold vision deck light on numbers doesn't survive the board.</strong> I don't doubt the ambition — I doubt the payback. Without revenue retained and a cost-to-serve change I can verify, the “digital-leadership narrative” is just spend with a story attached.`,

